I haven't been back in many many years;
So this review is really old. However, my experience was phenomenal. Great service, drinks and food.
But the attention to service was fabulous!

Great place to splurge or celebrate a special occasion. Or go for a regular dinner if you are baller like that. 

Ranked #1 restaurant in 2017 worldwide and #4 restaurant in 2018, it definitely shows in your experience there. The 3 Michelin stars don't hurt either. 

// LOCATION // across from Madison Square Park (not garden!)

// SERVICE // impeccable, while casual and not pretentious. You never feel like it's a stuffy environment which we loved. 

// FOOD // 10 courses focused on seasonal offerings and smattered with fun surprises along the way. We had the spring menu and we loved some of the Asian influences including the use of black sesame and ginger.

You might enjoy this restaurant very much if you:
(1) have tried more than 10 Michelin restaurants in the past and can tell the difference between any two of them;
(2) care a great deal about the quality of service in a restaurant (which means it only takes one unfriendly or super-snobbish waiter to completely ruin your appetite);
(3) like simple but elegant decoration;
(4) are often curious about how a meal is prepared and/or are a good chef yourself.

The dining experience at EMP is a game that goes on for 3 hours. There's only one tasting menu (if you sit in the dining room, not the bar) and it lasts for 3 hours. At multiple points in the process, you sit and watch the waiters complete the last steps in the preparation of a course at your table. For me, that was the fun part. However, if you are pushed for time and really don't take an interest in seeing the behind-the-scene steps, maybe you'll think this process was extraneous.

We appreciated the service very much. All the waiters seemed friendly and genuinely knowledgeable about the courses. Chatting with them occasionally between courses was truly enjoyable. Why can't all waiters be like them?

What we thought could be improved was that we didn't get a taste of real wagyu tartare. The restaurant used carrots instead of wagyu for the tartare (perhaps out of a concern for "eating healthy"). As a meat-lover, I couldn't help thinking how the course would've tasted if it used real wagyu, although I loved the carrot tartare course just fine.

The other thing was that in a cheese course which was prepared as a picnic basket, the grapes we had weren't tasty at all. It's minor, but I thought I should point it out nevertheless.

After feasting at Per Se last night, I was prepared to be a little underwhelmed at Eleven Madison Park. Happily, I was not. The four-course tasting was flawlessly executed and the ingredients popped. Some might complain that a few of Daniel Humm's dishes echoed each other in preparation (e.g., a few too many pureed root vegetables or dehydrated-then-rehydrated fruits), but I enjoyed every single item that came out of the kitchen.

The only minor glitch: At the start of the meal, our server asked us if we had any dietary restrictions. My wife hesitantly asked if we could go grain- and potato-free. "Not a problem!" exclaimed our waiter. "This is precisely why we don't specify any specific preparations on the menu -- we can be flexible and prepare the meal to your wishes." Excellent!

But then the amuse bouche arrived: Roasted potato tea with rosemary, sage and thyme, served alongside a potato crisp with dehydrated sour cream, creme fraiche and chive. And then the waiter came back with bread. But it was all wonderful, so we didn't care that the server didn't know the definition of "grains." Or "potatoes."

Our tasting menus then began in earnest. My wife and I each picked different items for our meals so that we'd have eight plates to taste:

Foie gras torchon, inset with braised wild boar and served with figs, compressed melon, microgreens, beets and turnips -- this dish was an explosion of color and flavor;

Avocado roulade stuffed with crab meat, apple, and creme fraiche -- delicate, creamy, smooth, mouth-filling;

Poached snapper with parsnip puree, dehydrated parsnip, poached Anjou pear, and Italian parsley;

Poached lobster with Jamon Iberico ham, fresh fig, white tea-infused fig, and sweet potato puree;

Sous vide pork two ways: roasted belly, and tenderloin finished in the oven, and served with dried and roasted plums, butternut squash puree, and leeks -- the roast pork belly in particular was insanely good;

Sous vide squab breast with pate, wine-braised red cabbage, Savoy cabbage, dried apple and apple puree;

Three goat cheeses from Andante in Petaluma: one with an ash ring, once infused with tarragon and sage, and one wrapped in grape leaves. All were excellent, and served with pistachios, greens, and nutty, seeded crisps;

Hazelnut ice cream, pistachio ice cream, hazelnut meringue, hazelnut brittle with grapes, thinly-shaved hazelnut, and chestnut mousse;

"PB&J": Raspberry pate de fruit covered with liquid shortbread, plus a peanut butter truffle;

My personal favorite: "Truffled truffle": Chocolate and truffle (the real kind -- or as the server put it: "truffle from the earth") -flavored ice cream, coated in chocolate crumbles;

Chocolate bonbons topped with three different salts and three different caramels.

Eleven Madison Park was pitch-perfect, and although we had our fill of sugar near the end of the meal, it wasn't enough to wipe us out for dinner. Which is fortunate, because Momofuku Ko was up next...
